FAIL CONDITION: 2005.2, script step 19 (Security Menu '1. Set security Level to 1
  (None)'). After the device's own warning
      "WARNING: This option will reset the security level to One.
       which will DESTROY the entire contents of the FLASH file system"
  the script answers y and waits 30 s for its expected text:
      send(y, 30, ['Erasing flash', 'Security Settings menu']) wait time reached
  The device's actual output in that window:
      "  Erasing nand0:       [                                                  ]<ESC>[51D=================================================="
      "  Erase complete. Restarting..."
      (<ESC> = the raw escape byte in swi_a_2005.log)
  Neither expected string appeared, so the framework set
      !!FAIL: Device does not appear to be erasing flash after resetting security level
  The tester graded the TestSet FAIL because that FAIL condition was set by the
  device's output. The erase itself did happen: the unit then restarted in a loop
  ("Bootloader 6.2.40 loaded" / "Press <Ctrl+B> for the Boot Menu" x4), `sho boot`
  read "Current boot image : Not set" and "Current boot config: flash:/default.cfg
  (file not found)", and the Security Settings menu read "currently set to 1 (None)". So
  what failed is the erase message the script expects ("Erasing flash"), not the
  erase. 2005.2's second FAIL ("TestSet configuration file flash:/swi_a_5700_2005.cfg
  missing from swi_a") follows from that same erase: the TestCase tear-down tries to set
  the boot config back to a file the case's own step 19 had erased. It is a framework
  tear-down check, not a device check.
CAVEAT:  2005.3-2005.8 (Security Level 3, password length limits, valid password
  characters, level shown in `show boot`, password timeout, Start-Shell blocked at
  levels 2/3) were not exercised in this run. This run shows that the level-1 reset
  erases flash and resets the level. It does not establish whether the script's
  expected text 'Erasing flash' or the device's "Erasing nand0:" is the correct
  message. That is for the Test Engineer to decide.

